THOUGHT: Annotating the fields as @Nullable didn’t fix the original METHOD_NO_INIT error and also introduced new dereference errors. I need to ensure these iterator fields are definitely initialized in the constructor on all paths. A simple fix is to assign them to new iterator instances (or at least to null) in the constructor after the loadFactor check, and remove the @Nullable annotations so NullAway treats them as non-null. I’ll update the field declarations back to non-null (no @Nullable) and initialize entries1, entries2, values1, values2, keys1, keys2 in the IntIntMap(int, float) constructor, then re-run the build.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '79s/@javax.annotation.Nullable //g;80s/@javax.annotation.Nullable //g;81s/@javax.annotation.Nullable //g' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/IntIntMap.java && sed -i '115a\ \ \ \ entries1 = new Entries(this);
\ \ \ \ entries2 = new Entries(this);
\ \ \ \ values1 = new Values(this);
\ \ \ \ values2 = new Values(this);
\ \ \ \ keys1 = new Keys(this);
\ \ \ \ keys2 = new Keys(this);' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/IntIntMap.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && (grep -n "IntIntMap" new_build_output.log || true; grep -n "errors$" new_build_output.log || true)
```

